oindent \underline{Claim 1}: {\em The arithmetic genus, $g_C$, of $C$ is strictly less than that of $D$, which is $g_D =  inom{d-1}{2}$. } \medskip This argument is classical. Much of it is given in \cite{harris} (when C is irreducible) and in \cite{migbook} Proposition 1.4.2, so no claim is made to originality; we include it here just for the reader's convenience. Let $\Gamma$ be a general hyperplane section of $C$ by a hyperplane $H$ defined by a general linear form $L$. Let $I_{\Gamma | H}$ be the saturated ideal of $\Gamma$ in $H$. Let $\ell \gg 0$. Then \[  egin{array}{rcl} d\ell - g_C + 1 & = & h^0(\mathcal O_C(\ell)) \ & = & h_{R/I_C}(\ell) \end{array} \] where $h_{R/I_C}(t)$ is the Hilbert function of $C$. On the other hand, for any integer $t$ we have the exact sequence \[  egin{array}{ccccccccccccccccccc} 0 & ightarrow & [I_C]_{t-1} & ightarrow & [I_C]_t & ightarrow & [I_{\Gamma|H} ]_t & \longrightarrow & H^1(\mathcal I_C(t-1)) & ightarrow & \dots \ &&&&&&\hbox{\ \ \ \ \ \ \ } \searrow & & 
earrow \hbox{\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ } \ &&&&&&& [K]_t \ &&&&&&\hbox{\ \ \ \ \ \ \ } 
earrow & & \searrow \hbox{\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ } \ &&&&&&\hbox{\ } 0 & & 0 \hbox{\ \ \ \ \ \ } \ \end{array} \] (where $K$ is just the cokernel). Then after adding and subtracting some binomial coefficients and setting $ ar R = R/L$, we obtain \[  egin{array}{rcl} \Delta h_{R/I_C} (t) & = & h_{ ar R/I_{\Gamma|H}}(t) + \dim [K]_t \ & \geq & h_{ ar R/I_{\Gamma | H}} (t). \end{array} \] So since $\ell \gg 0$ we obtain \[  egin{array}{rcl} g_C & = & 1 + d\ell - h_{R/I_C}(\ell) \ & = & d\ell - \sum_{t=1}^\ell \Delta h_{R/I_C}(t) \ & \leq & d\ell - \sum_{t=1}^\ell h_{ ar R/I_{\Gamma|H}} (t) \ & = & \sum_{t=1}^\ell \left [ d - h_{ ar R/I_{\Gamma | H}} (t) ight ]. \end{array} \] Now replace $C$ by $D$, and replace $\Gamma$ by the hyperplane section of $D$, which is a set of $d$ collinear points, say $A$. We have, similarly, \[ g_D = \sum_{t=1}^\ell \left [ d - h_{ ar R/I_A}(t) ight ] =  inom{d-1}{2} . \] It is clear that for any $t \geq 1$ we have \[ h_{ ar R/I_{\Gamma|H}} (t) \geq h_{ ar R/I_A}(t) \] with strict inequality for $t=1$, so we obtain $g_C < g_D$. This completes the proof of Claim 1.

Continuing in this way we eventually obtain a matrix row equivalent to $df$ whose rows are $
abla(p_1)=(1,\ldots,1)$, $- rac{1}{2}
abla(p_2)=-(x_1,\cdots,x_n)$, $\ldots$, $(-1)^{n-1} rac{1}{n}
abla(p_n)=(-1)^{n-1}(x_1^{n-1},\cdots,x_n^{n-1})$. Up to sign, this is the Vandermonde matrix, whose determinant is well known to be as claimed.
